  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 201 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nRain chances continue to Tuesday through Wednesday night, with \nlocally heavy rainfall expected, especially across southwest and \ncentral Nebraska. Throughout Monday, Hurricane Polo will continue on \nits northeast track, gradually weakening as it encounters the \nsouthern jet stream. As the trough in the southern jet continues \ntracking east, it will begin to siphon moisture from Polo tracking \nit across the Plains. This will provide the second push of deep, \ntropical moisture across the Plains. However, there does remain some \nslight uncertainty in how far north and west this plume of moisture \ntracks. The European solution remains just a bit quicker than the \nGFS, which impacts the western extent of the moisture plume. \nHowever, there is strong consensus on this tracking across the \nPlains, bringing heavy rainfall. For our area specifically, \nconfidence is highest along and south of a Grant to Broken Bow line. \nEnsemble guidance continues to suggest a strong possibility for \nheavier rainfall in the area as well. Latest ensemble guidance \nbrings a 50 to 70 percent chance of total rainfall exceeding one \ninch across southwest Nebraska, with some locations even reaching an \n80 percent chance of exceedance. Ensembles also show now a 30 to 50 \npercent chance of total rainfall exceeding 2 inches, with the \nEuropean ensembles on the higher end of probabilities. Further \nbuilding confidence in this is the latest EFI/SoT, indicating \nanomalous rainfall totals across southwest into northeast Nebraska. \nWith this growing confidence in heavy rainfall, WPC has expanded the \nSlight Risk for Excessive Rainfall into portions of southwest and \ncentral Nebraska, with a Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall \nroughly along and south of an Ogallala to O'Neill line. All this to \nsay, we could see some very heavy rainfall and potentially some \nlocalized flooding mid week. Will continue to keep an eye on these \nforecast trends; however, as seen over the last few days, confidence \ncontinues to build in heavy rainfall this week. Make sure to stay \nweather aware! \n\nThursday into the weekend, the upper low slowly tracks east, \nremaining south of the region. This is expected to pull in some \ncooler air from Canada, which is trending us towards a cooler end of \nthe week. Highs now are expected to remain in the 60s, which is \nslightly cooler than our normal highs around 71 to 72 degrees for \nearly October. This cooler push may bring our overnight lows closer \nto seasonal however, as lows Thursday night onward are now expected \nto drop into the mid to upper 40s. This does remain slightly above \nour normal lows for early October, as we normally see lows around 41 \nto 43 degrees. However, this will be cooler than our recent stretch \nof lows in the 50s. Those with outdoor plants or animals may want to \ntake precaution as we continue to cool off heading into October.",
